3. EQUIVALENCE PROOF (why the grid version cannot miss an in-range pair)
================================================================================
Lemma: for real a, b with |a - b| < T, |floor(a/T) - floor(b/T)| <= 1.
Proof: let x = a/T, y = b/T, so |x - y| < 1. WLOG x <= y, y - x < 1. Then
  floor(y) <= floor(x + 1) = floor(x) + 1   (floor monotone; floor(x+1)=floor(x)+1)
and floor(y) >= floor(x) trivially, so 0 <= floor(y) - floor(x) <= 1. Symmetric
for y < x. QED.

Applied per axis: Euclidean distance < snap_tol  =>  each |coord diff| < snap_tol
=> each axis key differs by at most 1  =>  the two vertices lie in the same or an
adjacent cell, i.e. inside the 27-cell neighbourhood. Every pair the O(n^2) loop
would merge is therefore examined; the distance predicate is the same bit-exact
float64 arithmetic (coords[high] - coords[low], then diff @ diff), so the merge
verdict for every pair is identical. Edge set equal  =>  connected components
equal  =>  identical cluster partition.

Canonical root: the "smaller root absorbs larger" rule preserves the invariant
root(x) = min{indices in component of x} at every step, so the canonical vertex of
each cluster is its smallest member index — independent of union order. Hence the
output remap is deterministic and in fact BIT-IDENTICAL to the O(n^2) version
(plan allowed canonical choice to differ; it does not).

Boundary case (dist exactly = snap_tol): keys still differ by <= 1
(floor(x+1) = floor(x)+1), so the pair is probed and rejected by the strict
< snap_sq predicate — same as the old code. Covered by the new boundary test.

